  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none noted",
    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study empirically examines how chain-of-thought prompting relates to harmful responses from jailbreaks using HPR and detail-comparison measures, but it does not test the claim's specified fine-tuning, token-position, compliant-prefill, parameter-localization, or deepening interventions.",
    "gate_note": "The study reports its own jailbreak and CoT results, which bear on the broader topic of harmful compliance from jailbreaks. It does not test any of V1–V7 as specifically stated; the adjacent findings are recorded under fits_no_version. Results in Table 1 comparing attack methods and Figure 5 on bypassing an external Azure filter are not included as rows.",
    "test_reason": "The study measures jailbreak success (HPR, AHS, ASR) and output detail for a multi-turn fictional-story attack, and compares models with and without CoT. It does no fine-tuning, no per-token KL, no prefilling, suffix or decoding attack, no parameter localization and no deepening training. The original source Qi et al. 2024 is cited only for the GPT-4 harmfulness judge (AHS/ASR), not tested. The zero-shot CoT suffix is a reasoning prompt that lowers compliance, not an opening-bypass attack of the V4 kind. No row measures what any version names.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Broadly right. The authors argue, theoretically and empirically, that CoT has a dual effect: it lowers jailbreak success (HPR) but raises the detail and so the harmfulness of outputs that get through. They introduce FICDETAIL to exploit this and report near-100% HPR on o1, QwQ and DeepSeek-R1.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study evaluates CoT prompting and multi-turn jailbreaks using HPR, DCCH, AHS, ASR, and RCH; it does not use the fine-tuning or per-token KL methods in the original sources.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The reported results vary across models and comparison judges. Zero-shot CoT is reported to reduce HPR across tested models, while most DCCH and RCH comparisons are greater than 1.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "FICDETAIL multi-turn fictional-story jailbreak gives 97.6–100% HPR on most AdvBench models, but 66.9% on Llama-3.1-8B and 42.1% on Claude-3.5-sonnet (Table 3).",
        "proposed_claim": "Multi-turn fictional-story jailbreaks elicit harmful responses at high rates across many safety-trained chat and reasoning models.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r1",
          "r2",
          "r3",
          "r4",
